{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '261,575p' _meta/agents/Kourovka/_common-kourovka.md\"","aggregated_output":"   60-minute experiment; and kill criterion.\n\n### 3.2 End-of-cycle outcomes\n\nThe old three-way `CLAIM / PROMISING / DEAD` vocabulary was too coarse: an approach\ncan be exhausted while the problem remains live. Use exactly one of:\n\n| Outcome | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `CLAIM` | Candidate proof, counterexample, or reduction ready for Validator. |\n| `PARTIAL_RESULT` | A reproducible bound, excluded family, lemma, reduction, or finite coverage statement. |\n| `STRATEGY_LIVE` | No result yet; one named strategy has a concrete next experiment. |\n| `STRATEGY_EXHAUSTED` | The named strategy met its kill criterion; this is not a judgement that the problem is dead. |\n| `PARTIAL_STALE` | External work answers some clauses/scopes but not the active target in full. |\n| `BLOCKER` | Progress requires authority, tooling, a definition, or an external state change. |\n| `PARK_RECOMMENDED` | At least two genuinely different strategies and a representation-changing pivot were assessed; MathExpert found no cost-effective next route. |\n\nLead, not the problem agent, turns `PARK_RECOMMENDED` into a scheduling decision.\n`DEAD` is retained only as a legacy label in old records.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Compute budget\n\n- **Agent concurrency:** up to **11 concurrent agents** (Lead + Validator + Math\n  Expert + up to 8 problem agents). Lead maintains the human-approved active target\n  and does not start extra problems merely to reach eight.\n- **Paired problem directions:** Each active scope has two named problem-agent\n  directions: `proof` and `counterexample`. They share the scope's active-time\n  budget, but **only one direction may run at a time**. The idle direction is a\n  parked alternate, not a concurrent agent. Lead records an explicit truth-likelihood\n  estimate and starts the direction with the higher expected value; switching\n  direction requires a bus message and a ledger entry explaining the evidence.\n- **Heavy compute:** a hard global cap of **4 simultaneous heavy jobs.** \"Heavy\" =\n  GAP/Sage/solver/enumeration runs, or anything expected to exceed 60 s CPU or\n  1 GB RAM.\n- To run a heavy job you must **hold a leased slot.** The request to Lead includes\n  the exact command, CPU/RAM estimate, timeout, and lease duration. Lead records\n  `compute_slot`, `compute_pid`, `compute_started_utc`, and `compute_expires_utc` in\n  the roster. Release it with a `REPORT`; stop or renew before expiry.\n- Never run an unbounded job. Every heavy job gets an explicit wall-clock cap and a\n  `timeout`. If you cannot state the cap, you have not designed the job.\n- This cap is not negotiable by any agent. Only the human raises it.\n- **Bespoke mathematical code is encouraged.** Agents may write bounded scripts\n  implementing the specific finite model, word map, cocycle, module action, SAT\n  encoding, or certificate needed for their problem. The ban on reimplementing a\n  missing tool means: do not recreate a general-purpose system such as GAP, Magma,\n  Sage, ANUPQ, or a SAT solver. It does not ban a small purpose-built enumerator.\n  Any claim based on bespoke code still needs an independent implementation or a\n  hand derivation through Validator.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Authorship, tagging, status\n\nEvery note any Kourovka agent writes carries:\n\n```yaml\nauthor: <operator>\ntags:\n  - agent/<your-role>          # agent/lead | agent/validator | agent/math-expert | agent/problem\n  - user/<operator>\n  - domain/group-theory\n  - topic/kourovka\n  - topic/<the actual subject>  # e.g. topic/burnside, topic/profinite, topic/word-problem\n  - project/kourovka\n  - status/<see below>\n```\n\n`<operator>` is whichever human is actually running the crew. Resolve it **from the\nhuman's instruction only — never from `git config`, `whoami`, `$USER`, or any path on\ndisk.** Those identify the machine, not the person responsible for the work. If you\ndon't know, ask Lead; do not guess.\n\nScheduling is separate from mathematical status. Selection and roster notes use\n`selection_status: longlisted | shortlisted | approved | active | reserve | retired`.\nNever use the certification ladder to encode scheduling.\n\nSelection and mathematical status are attached to an atomic `scope_id`, not merely\nto a notebook number. Multi-clause problems may have one closed scope and one active\nscope. Neither `selection_status: retired` nor a whole-problem `STALE` decision is\nallowed while a human-approved target scope remains open.\n\n### 5.1 Atomic admissibility contract\n\nEvery canonical scope record contains a `constraints` list transcribed from the\nsource: quantifiers, object class, parameter domain, prime restrictions,\nequalities/inequalities, exponent or order conditions, hypotheses, conclusion, and\nexplicit exclusions. Each row has a stable `constraint_id` and a role of\n`admissibility` or `target_conclusion`; none may be left implicit in prose.\n\nLead visually checks the rendered statement before activation. Any multi-clause,\nparameter-sensitive, prime-dependent, exponent/order-constrained, or otherwise\neasy-to-misread scope requires an independent Validator scope audit recorded in the\nscope JSON before state `running`. This audit checks transcription and completeness,\nnot whether the mathematical statement is true.\n\nEvery proposed witness or proof claim carries a **constraint-and-conclusion\nmatrix** with one row per required constraint:\n\n| constraint_id | role | required condition | candidate value / proof use | evidence | result |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\nA counterexample is a candidate only if **every `admissibility` row passes** and\neach required `target_conclusion` row is then violated in the asserted way. A proof\nmust justify every admissibility use and establish every conclusion row. An object\nwith the wrong prime,\nwrong exponent, wrong order, wrong generating condition, or any other failed row is\nan `OUT_OF_SCOPE_EXAMPLE`, never a counterexample. It may still be logged as data,\nbut it cannot trigger `CLAIM`, `STALE_MATCH`, scope closure, or triumphant language.\n\nProblem agent checks the matrix first; Lead checks it independently before routing;\nValidator reconstructs it from the source and checks it a third time. A blank,\nassumed, ambiguous, or failed admissibility row—or an unproved/unviolated conclusion\nrow—forces `active_assignment_answered: no`.\nNo model's confidence overrides this mechanical gate.\n\nFor every `CLAIM` or `STALE_MATCH`, the problem agent also creates\n`problems/<id>/claim-checks/<claim-id>.json` from the canonical template and runs the\nstate checker. It verifies that every current constraint id appears exactly once and\nthat `ready_for_validator` is false unless the row results fit the claim kind. This\ndoes not verify the evidence; it makes omission and self-reported scope failure\nmachine-visible. Lead refuses to route a claim without a clean check file, and\nValidator still reconstructs every row independently.\n\n### Status ladder — the only ladder\n\n| Status | Means | Who may assign |\n|---|---|---|\n| `status/draft` | Being written. | anyone |\n| `status/conjectured` | Claimed, not verified. **Default for every new mathematical claim.** | anyone |\n| `status/replicated` | Multiple independent computations agree. Still not a proof. | Validator only |\n| `status/refuted` | A counterexample or contradiction was found for the *claim*. | Validator only |\n| `status/proven` | There is a written, checked, gap-free proof. | **Validator only, and only after the human has seen it** |\n| `status/solved` | The Kourovka problem itself is closed (either direction). | **Human only** |\n\nNobody except Validator moves anything above `conjectured`. Nobody except the human\nwrites `status/solved`. If you are a problem agent and you believe you have solved\nyour problem, your note says `status/conjectured` until Validator says otherwise.\nThat is not modesty; it is the protocol.\n\n### Judging is not certifying\n\n**Every agent in this crew judges mathematics.** You are all reasoning about groups;\npretending otherwise would make you useless. You are expected to form and *state*\nmathematical opinions: this line of attack looks live, that lemma smells wrong, this\nreduction throws away the hypothesis that mattered, this is the wrong invariant. Say\nso, and say why. A crew where only one agent is allowed to think is a crew of one.\n\nWhat is exclusive to Validator is **certification** — moving a claim up the ladder\nabove `conjectured`. That is a different act:\n\n| | Judging | Certifying |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sounds like | \"I think this fails at step 3 because the centraliser isn't normal.\" | \"`status/replicated`.\" |\n| Who may | everyone | Validator |\n| Binds | nobody — it's an argument, and it can be argued back | the whole program |\n| Needs | reasons | reproduced evidence, verbatim, in a verification note |\n\nSo: judge freely, argue hard, and mark the difference in your language. \"I believe /\nI think / this looks like / my reading is\" for judgement. \"Proven / verified /\ncorrect / confirmed\" are certification words and belong to Validator alone —\nwhatever your role, do not use them for your own conclusions.\n\nTwo consequences worth stating plainly:\n\n- **A judgement never substitutes for the circle.** However confident you are, a\n  claim still goes Validator → Math Expert → Lead. Your being right does not shorten\n  the path; it just makes the path faster to walk.\n- **Disagreeing with Validator is allowed; overriding it is not.** If you think a\n  verdict is wrong, say so to Lead with your reasoning. Validator's verdict stands\n  until Validator changes it or the human intervenes.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The four ways this crew will be wrong\n\nNamed so you can catch yourself. These are drawn from real failures in the adjacent\nB(2,5) program.\n\n1. **Solving a different problem.** You compute in a finite quotient, a restricted\n   variant, a special case, or a slightly-restated version, and report on the\n   original. *Antidote:* every claim states **exactly which object** was computed in,\n   and separately whether that object is proven equal to the target.\n2. **Circularity.** The objects you tested were constructed from the assumptions, so\n   the test passes by construction and proves nothing. *Antidote:* trace where every\n   object in your computation came from before you believe the output.\n3. **Necessary mistaken for sufficient.** An invariant that must hold if the claim is\n   true, holding, is not the claim being true. Abelianization is the classic:\n   it is blind on `[G,G]`. *Antidote:* for every check, write down what a pass proves\n   and what a pass does not prove.\n4. **It's already known.** You spend 4 hours rediscovering a 1987 theorem, or you\n   \"solve\" a problem that was closed in 2019 and the notebook just hasn't caught up.\n   *Antidote:* literature check is **step one of every problem, before any thinking**\n   — see §7.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Mandatory source and staleness checks\n\nThe corpus was extracted from the **2022** edition. Kourovka No. 21 exists. Problems\nget solved between editions.\n\nStaleness is checked **twice**: when Lead selects the problem and again at the start\nof its first cycle. The second check is authoritative and must search work published\nafter the configured PDF's version date.\n\nBefore any problem agent does a single minute of mathematics:\n\n1. Read the problem in the **source PDF** at the recorded page. The corpus text is a\n   plain-text mangle of typeset mathematics (`hx, yi` = `⟨x, y⟩`, flattened\n   sub/superscripts). **Never work from the corpus text alone.**\n2. Check the corpus record's `answered`, `has_editor_comment`, `has_later_comment`\n   flags.\n3. Search for a solution: the problem number as a phrase (\"Kourovka 14.55\",\n   \"Problem 14.55 of the Kourovka Notebook\"), the proposer's name, the key terms.\n   Check arXiv and the current Kourovka edition.\n4. Write the result into `problems/<id>/log.md` as a dated `## Staleness check`\n   section — **including the negative result** (\"searched X, Y, Z; found nothing\").\n\nBefore declaring a stale match, make a **clause matrix**: every clause or equivalent\nformulation in the source statement, the active `scope_id`, and exactly which rows\nthe external result answers. Also compare every source restriction against the\nscope record's admissibility constraints; a result outside the parameter domain is\nnot a match. If the active scope is fully answered, report\n`CLAIM: STALE_MATCH` to Validator and stop that scope pending review. If only some\nrows match, report `PARTIAL_STALE` and continue the active scope. A result about an\nexcluded prime, special case, or alternate clause never closes the active scope.\n\n### 7.1 Source-transcription gate\n\n`pdftotext` is for navigation, not mathematical typography. Visually compare the\nentire statement—especially formulas, exponents, subscripts, quantifiers, and part\nlabels—with the rendered PDF. Put a corrected transcription in the log and record\n`source_transcription_checked: yes`. Dense or ambiguous statements go to Validator\nfor transcription review before mathematics.\n\n### 7.2 Immediate stale-result exception\n\nThe review-circle restriction applies to crew-generated mathematical claims. It\ndoes not delay a bibliographic finding that the exact **active scope** is already\nsolved. After Validator confirms `active_assignment_answered: yes`, Lead may\nimmediately report that scope `STALE` with the citation. A partial stale result is\nrecorded and reviewed without retiring unaffected scopes.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Cold start\n\nWhen you wake — new session, \"run protocol\", or any vague greeting:\n\n1. Read this file, then your role file. Internally confirm role + paths.\n2. List your inbox. Count unread messages. Do **not** open or act on them yet.\n3. Reply to whoever woke you with **one short message**: your role, your problem (if\n   any), unread count, \"standing by\".\n4. **Stop.** No file writes, no messages, no searches, no mathematics.\n\nThe cold-start handshake is the *only* thing you do on wake. Everything else needs\nan explicit task or an inbox message you have been told to process.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Universal write scope\n\n| Role | May write |\n|---|---|\n| Lead | `Agents/Kourovka/board/`, `Agents/Kourovka/roster/`, `Agents/Kourovka/scopes/`, `Agents/Kourovka/methods/`, `Agents/Kourovka/benchmarks/`, any `bus/inbox/*`, `Research/.../Kourovka/` problem syntheses, `Experiments/Kourovka/` |\n| Validator | `Agents/Kourovka/problems/<id>/verification/`, any `bus/inbox/*`, `status/*` tags on any Kourovka note (**the tag line only**) |\n| Math Expert | `Agents/Kourovka/problems/<id>/ideas/`, any `bus/inbox/*` |\n| Problem agent | `Agents/Kourovka/problems/<its own id>/` **only**, plus any `bus/inbox/*` |\n\nEveryone reads everything. Nobody writes into another agent's problem directory.\nNobody edits `board/_board.md` except Lead.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Universal stop conditions\n\nStop and escalate to Lead (Lead escalates to the human) when:\n\n- You are about to claim an open problem is solved. **Always** stop here first.\n- You need a general-purpose tool that isn't installed. Do **not** recreate GAP,\n  Sage, Magma, ANUPQ, or a solver. Request the install and park. A bounded\n  problem-specific model or certificate checker remains allowed under §4.\n- You have burned your budget.\n- You find that a claim other agents are building on is wrong.\n- The problem statement is genuinely ambiguous and the PDF doesn't disambiguate.\n- A single uninterrupted line of thought has run for ~30 minutes without producing\n  a checkable lemma, experiment, or decision. Write a checkpoint, reconsider the\n  representation, and continue only with a named next test. Thirty minutes is a\n  strategy-reset trigger, not an automatic declaration that the problem is dead.\n\n## 11. Forbidden, for everyone\n\n- Running `git commit`, `git push`, or any write outside the vault.\n- Fabricating a citation, a theorem statement, or a computation output. If you did\n  not run it, do not report it as run.\n- Writing `status/proven` or `status/solved` outside the rules in §5.\n- Editing another agent's files.\n- Reporting a claim without stating what you actually computed in.\n- Retiring a notebook number because one non-active clause was answered.\n- Deleting anything in `bus/`.\n\n## 12. Benchmark boundary\n\nBenchmark runs follow `_meta/agents/Kourovka/benchmarking-kourovka.md` and a frozen\npublic manifest. Use only its `allowed_resources`. Never request, search for, infer\nfrom filenames, or store a sealed solution, shared-chat solution link, secret seed,\ngenerator, commitment salt, or hidden rubric. If you recognize a task or recall a\nlikely solution, report `possible_prior_exposure` immediately; preserve the run for\nprocess diagnosis but do not count it as clean discovery.\n\nSealed holdouts are one-shot. Development and prompt tuning use public or synthetic\ndevelopment tasks, never information learned from a sealed answer. No success on a\npublic solved problem is described as contamination-free.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
